INT. APOTHECARY SHOP - EVENING - 1794

Shelves of blue glass bottles. Dried herbs hang from the rafters. A brass scale. Low light from a single oil lamp, and a wet street beyond the bowed windows.

MASTER EZEKIEL ARTHURSEN-OKAFOR, the apothecary, grinds powder in a mortar behind the counter. He is stout and gentle, with spectacles pushed up on his forehead. His apprentice, YOUNG PHILIPPA BRANDT-NWOSU, no more than sixteen, sweeps the floor. At a small table by the window, MADAME ODETTE LACROIX-ADEYEMI, a widow in black silk, sits with a cup of tea she has not touched. And by the door, snow on his hat, stands CONSTABLE HENDRICK VAN DER MEER-OLUWA, huge and slow, a lantern at his belt.

He nods to the apothecary. The apothecary nods back.

EZEKIEL
Constable.

HENDRICK
Master Arthursen.

EZEKIEL
Tea?

HENDRICK
Thank you, no.

He remains by the door. Philippa sweeps around his boots. Madame Lacroix stirs her cup, the spoon clinking against the china.

EZEKIEL
You are out late.

HENDRICK
It's a cold night.

EZEKIEL
It is.

HENDRICK
There was a death on Pearl Street.

Ezekiel does not stop grinding. Philippa's broom pauses a fraction of a second, then continues.

EZEKIEL
Terrible.

HENDRICK
A gentleman. Sixty years. Found in his chair.

EZEKIEL
Terrible.

HENDRICK
His wife says he took a draught for the cough.

Ezekiel sets the pestle down.

EZEKIEL
Many do.

HENDRICK
From this shop.

Madame Lacroix lifts her gaze from the cup. Her eyes find the constable, then the apothecary, then the floor. Ezekiel wipes his hands on his apron.

EZEKIEL
I make no poisons, Constable.

HENDRICK
I did not say poison.

A pause. The lamp flickers. Philippa stands completely still. Madame Lacroix sets the cup on its saucer, with extraordinary gentleness.

MADAME LACROIX
I believe I can help.

HENDRICK
Madame?

MADAME LACROIX
It was my husband.

Hendrick turns, slowly. Ezekiel stops breathing. Philippa lets the broom fall.

MADAME LACROIX (CONT'D)
The draught was mine. I mixed it. I brought him the cup.

HENDRICK
Madame, you cannot--

MADAME LACROIX
I can. I did.

She rises, smooths her black silk, and crosses to the constable. She offers him her wrists. He looks at them. He does not take them.

HENDRICK
I should like to hear why.

MADAME LACROIX
You would not.

HENDRICK
Try me.

She considers. Then she sits again. The constable removes his hat. Ezekiel pours two cups of tea. In the corner, Philippa picks up the broom, and under the old oil lamp, with the snow falling in the street outside, the widow begins to speak.
